      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-333722",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the system $x^{\u2032}(t) = Ax(t)$, where $A$ is a constant $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix. Let $\u03a6(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $\u03a6(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $\u03a6(t)$\n> $L2$: $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n> $L3$: $det(\u03a6(t)) \\ne 0$ for all $t$\n> $L4$: $\u03a6(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from $L1$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from line $L2$ by {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from line {{field-3}} by the properties of invertible matrices."
    },
